## 1. Scenario Matrix Design

### 1.1 Objectives

- **Diversity**: Generate data from MANY different scenarios to ensure generalization
- **Balance**: 2/3 attacks (split between negative and positive), 1/3 normal
- **Coverage**: Varied parameters to handle unseen future scenarios

### 1.2 Scenario Parameters

| Parameter | Description | Values | Rationale |
|-----------|-------------|--------|-----------|
| **Scenario Type** | Attack vs Normal | normal, positive, negative | Core classification task |
| **Random Seed** | RNG initialization | 1-100 | Different realizations of same scenario |
| **Attack Bias** | Backoff manipulation | -10000 to +10000 | Attack intensity variation |
| **Number of Stations** | Network size | 2, 3, 4 | Different network densities |
| **Data Rate** | Application traffic | 40, 60, 80, 100 Mbps | Different traffic loads |
| **Simulation Length** | Duration | 600s, 900s, 1200s | Different observation windows |

### 1.3 Scenario Matrix (300 Total Files)

#### Normal Traffic (100 files = 33.3%)

| Configuration | Count | Description |
|--------------|-------|-------------|
| **Baseline** (2 sta, 60 Mbps, 900s) | 40 | Standard configuration, seeds 1-40 |
| **High Load** (3 sta, 100 Mbps, 900s) | 20 | High traffic density, seeds 41-60 |
| **Low Load** (2 sta, 40 Mbps, 600s) | 20 | Low traffic, shorter duration, seeds 61-80 |
| **Dense Network** (4 sta, 80 Mbps, 900s) | 20 | More stations, seeds 81-100 |

#### Positive Attack (100 files = 33.3%)

| Bias Value | Count | Configuration | Seeds |
|------------|-------|---------------|-------|
| +1000 | 15 | 2 sta, 60 Mbps, 900s | 1-15 |
| +2500 | 15 | 2 sta, 60 Mbps, 900s | 16-30 |
| +5000 | 20 | 2 sta, 60 Mbps, 900s | 31-50 |
| +7500 | 15 | 3 sta, 80 Mbps, 900s | 51-65 |
| +10000 | 15 | 3 sta, 80 Mbps, 900s | 66-80 |
| +5000 (long) | 10 | 2 sta, 60 Mbps, 1200s | 81-90 |
| +5000 (dense) | 10 | 4 sta, 100 Mbps, 900s | 91-100 |

#### Negative Attack (100 files = 33.3%)

| Bias Value | Count | Configuration | Seeds |
|------------|-------|---------------|-------|
| -1000 | 15 | 2 sta, 60 Mbps, 900s | 1-15 |
| -2500 | 15 | 2 sta, 60 Mbps, 900s | 16-30 |
| -5000 | 20 | 2 sta, 60 Mbps, 900s | 31-50 |
| -7500 | 15 | 3 sta, 80 Mbps, 900s | 51-65 |
| -10000 | 15 | 3 sta, 80 Mbps, 900s | 66-80 |
| -5000 (long) | 10 | 2 sta, 60 Mbps, 1200s | 81-90 |
| -5000 (dense) | 10 | 4 sta, 100 Mbps, 900s | 91-100 |

### 1.4 Why This Matrix?

**Diversity Sources**:
1. Random seeds (100 per scenario type) - different stochastic realizations
2. Attack intensities (7 bias levels) - from subtle to extreme
3. Network configurations (4 variants) - different densities and loads
4. Simulation durations (3 lengths) - different observation windows

**Balance Justification**:
- 33% normal: Sufficient to learn normal patterns without overwhelming attacks
- 67% attacks: Emphasizes security focus (precision recall for attack detection)
- 50/50 positive/negative: Equal representation of both attack types

**Parameter Ranges Rationale**:
- **Bias**: -10000 to +10000 covers full attack spectrum seen in original research
- **Stations**: 2-4 matches typical small WiFi networks
- **Data Rate**: 40-100 Mbps represents realistic application traffic
- **Duration**: 600-1200s (10-20 min) provides sufficient temporal data

